WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.860
It’s said that symmetry is fundamentally beautiful.

00:00:02.860 --> 00:00:04.180
But how fundamental is that?

00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:07.900
Hey Crazies.

00:00:07.900 --> 00:00:10.460
My life is usually pretty hectic in March, but it’s Women’s History Month,

00:00:10.460 --> 00:00:13.960
so I wanted to take some time to honor a very important woman in physics:

00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:14.920
Emmy Noether

00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:16.140
Wasn’t she a mathematician?

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:19.520
Technically, yes, but physics got to borrow her brilliance for a few days.

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:22.880
This story takes place in Guttingen, Germany in 1915

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:25.820
Einstein and Hilbert were trying to finish general relativity,

00:00:25.820 --> 00:00:27.380
but they ran into a little snag.

00:00:27.380 --> 00:00:31.040
If energy warps space-time and space-time contains energy,

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:33.480
then space-time should be able to warp space-time.

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:35.660
That’s what we call a paradox!

00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:39.399
They needed a fresh set of eyes, so they invited Noether to the university.

00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:42.860
By that time, she was already known for being a brilliant mathematician.

00:00:42.860 --> 00:00:44.960
I mean, here’s a list of her accomplishments.

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:48.129
But the invitation was met with such heavy resistance from the establishment

00:00:48.129 --> 00:00:50.989
that Hilbert had to advertise her lectures
under his name

00:00:50.989 --> 00:00:52.640
and list her as an “Assistant.”

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:53.960
Totally ridiculous!

00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:55.740
It’s a good thing they found a loophole though because,

00:00:55.740 --> 00:00:58.940
without Emmy Noether, general relativity may never have been finished.

00:00:58.940 --> 00:01:01.580
Right, relativity, we were talking about a paradox.

00:01:01.580 --> 00:01:04.500
It turns out not all energy warps space-time.

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:07.020
Just the stuff in this stress-energy tensor,

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:10.860
which is all the energy except the energy in space-time itself.

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:14.280
Space-time is on one side, everything else
is on the other.

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:18.320
This was a good enough explanation for a physicist, but Noether was a mathematician

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:19.720
and like a true mathematician:

00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:21.640
She generalized it to death!

00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:26.400
Total energy is what we call a conserved quantity,
which is a quantity that does not change.

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:30.740
As a result of her work on general relativity,
Noether notably noticed something.

00:01:30.740 --> 00:01:32.120
I’m such a dork.

00:01:32.120 --> 00:01:35.840
She noticed where all conserved quantities actually come from.

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:37.340
Symmetry!

00:01:37.340 --> 00:01:40.780
Now, just to keep things clear, we’re not talking about the visual symmetry,

00:01:40.780 --> 00:01:43.820
you see in things like organisms and playing cards.

00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:45.900
We’re talking about mathematical symmetry!

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:48.440
To demonstrate this, I’ve made some expendable clones.

00:01:48.440 --> 00:01:50.260
Hi! Wait, expendable?

00:01:50.260 --> 00:01:52.160
Well, we can’t exactly do this in the space station.

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:54.400
Don’t be ridiculous. Too many distractions.

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:04.900
Here’s a clone just floating in space.

00:02:04.900 --> 00:02:08.880
It’ll still be floating over here, or over here, or over here.

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:12.520
If the clone is moving in a straight line, it’ll do that here,

00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:14.180
or here, or here.

00:02:14.180 --> 00:02:17.860
That’s what we call translational symmetry and, in all of those situations,

00:02:17.860 --> 00:02:20.460
the clone’s linear momentum is conserved.

00:02:20.460 --> 00:02:23.840
The symmetry implies that linear momentum cannot change.

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:26.720
It’s even true if we have more than one clone in the system.

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:29.080
What’s happening doesn’t change if they’re here,

00:02:29.080 --> 00:02:30.760
or here, or here.

00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:33.620
Yes, we do lose the symmetry if we put them
over here,

00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:36.280
but then we also lose the momentum conservation.

00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:39.020
Unless, of course, we expand our system to
include the Earth.

00:02:39.020 --> 00:02:42.740
Rotational symmetry implies angular momentum can’t change.

00:02:42.740 --> 00:02:46.840
If the system of clones is here, but oriented
like this instead,

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:50.180
then their physics doesn’t change,
which is true if they’re like this

00:02:50.180 --> 00:02:52.400
or like this or like this!

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:57.520
Time symmetry implies energy can’t change, but that’s a little harder to show with clones.

00:02:57.520 --> 00:03:01.760
The idea is that you don’t have to set zero time at the Big Bang.

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:04.040
You can start your clock whenever you want.

00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:06.660
Which is why we can have things like time zones

00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:08.920
and we can switch our clocks to daylight savings time.

00:03:08.920 --> 00:03:11.020
Although, no one ever should, don’t get
me started.

00:03:11.020 --> 00:03:14.000
The main point here is that Emmy Noether was brilliant

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:16.560
and her theorem was incredibly insightful.

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:18.180
You might even say that it was beautiful.

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:21.460
And until next time, remember, it’s OK to
be a little crazy.

